            <ArticleTitle>Impact of haze from forest fire to respiratory health: Indonesian experience.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>169-74</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">This paper will describe the impact on the human lung of haze from forest fires in Indonesia based on data collected from different provinces.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODOLOGY" NlmCategory="METHODS">Data were collected from personal reports from pulmonologists working in the area as well as from province/district health offices and hospitals.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">These data show that there was a significant impact of haze to the human lung. There was a significant increase in respiratory conditions, lung function complaints and other related impacts.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Further studies, especially cohort studies, should be undertaken so that the long-term' impact of pollution from forest fires can be known.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
